<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Makhambet Otemisuly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1804–1846</span> · Also known as Makhambet Utemisov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Makhambet Otemisuly was a Kazakh poet and warrior of the Bukey Horde. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isatay-taimanuly">Isatay Taimanuly</a> he led the revolt of 1836–1838 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangir-khan">Jangir Khan</a> and the exactions of the Russian-backed elite, and his fiery verses became classics of Kazakh literature. After the revolt was crushed he lived as a fugitive and was murdered in 1846.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/makhambet-otemisuly.jpg" alt="Makhambet Otemisuly" width="210" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Post of Kazakhstan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stamp_of_Kazakhstan_399.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
